\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A coupled quartic spatial chain}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Take #{V=\{1,\ldots,s\}}, #{s\ge2}, with bonds
#{\{v,v+1\}}, common weight #{c>0} and free endpoints. For #{m>0}
and #{\lambda_v\ge0}, with at least one #{\lambda_v>0}, the
[slice potential](fdip-00GU) becomes
##{U(q)=\frac{m^2}{2}\sum_{v=1}^s q_v^2
 +\frac c2\sum_{v=1}^{s-1}(q_{v+1}-q_v)^2
 +\sum_{v=1}^s\frac{\lambda_vq_v^4}{24}.}
Every summand beyond the mass term is nonnegative, so
#{U(q)\ge(m^2/2)|q|^2}. Thus #{a=m^2/2,b=0} works for every
finite chain length. A single transfer couples two copies of this
whole chain through #{|x-y|^2/(2\epsilon)}; the spatial bonds are
already included inside each copy's potential.}

\p{For #{s=2}, the quadratic matrix is
##{K=\begin{pmatrix}m^2+c&-c\\-c&m^2+c\end{pmatrix}.}
The vectors #{(1,1)} and #{(1,-1)} have eigenvalues #{m^2} and
#{m^2+2c}. The quartic term couples the Gaussian modes through a
nonquadratic position weight. The [positive weighted laws](fdip-00GH)
give the finite-dimensional probability interpretation of this
onsite interaction.}
